R CMD INSTALL and R CMD SHLIB: make arguments in double quotes on Windows

install.R passes make variables on make's command line in single quotes
(SHLIB_LD='$(SHLIB_CXXLD)', CC='$(CC)', PKG_LIBS='...'). sh strips them on
unix, and so did MSYS2's make, whose cygwin runtime splits its command
line as sh does. A native Windows make (GNU make built for Windows32, the
native build tools of patch 0018 and r-zig-native-tools) splits it by the
C runtime's rules, which keep single quotes: a C++ or Fortran package that
make builds then fails ("No rule to make target '$(FCLIBS_XTRA)''", or a
link line naming "g++.exe -std=gnu++20  " as the program). Double quotes
work with both makes (kappa, 2026-11-24: MSYS2's make reads both forms
alike). Unix keeps single quotes, which stop sh from expanding $(...),
and so does a cross build on unix (R_CROSS_BUILD sets install.R's
WINDOWS, but its make runs under sh): the test is the host,
.Platform$OS.type.
A value ending in a backslash would escape the closing double quote under
the C runtime's rules; none of install.R's does. b13-design L4-F3,
b14-l4 PLAN.md.

--- a/src/library/tools/R/install.R
+++ b/src/library/tools/R/install.R
